Step 1: Create the Cluster Config
First, create a file named cluster.yaml. This ensures your laptop can "talk" to the nodes inside Docker.

YAML
kind: Cluster
apiVersion: kind.x-k8s.io/v1alpha4
nodes:
- role: control-plane
  extraPortMappings:
  - containerPort: 30001
    hostPort: 30001
- role: worker
- role: worker
Step 2: Spin up the Cluster
Run this command to build your three nodes (1 control-plane, 2 workers):

Bash
kind create cluster --config cluster.yaml
Wait for the terminal to say "Started cluster".

Step 3: Create the Pods (The Deployment)
You need an actual application for the Service to talk to. Create a file named deployment.yaml. Notice the labels match your service.

YAML
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: demo-app
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      env: demo
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        env: demo
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: nginx:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 80
Apply it:

Bash
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
Step 4: Create the Service
Now, apply your nodeport.yaml file to open the "door" to those pods.

Bash
kubectl apply -f nodeport.yaml
Step 5: Verify Everything
Now we check if the components are talking to each other.

Check Nodes: kubectl get nodes (Should see 3 nodes).

Check Pods: kubectl get pods (Should see 2 pods running).

Check Service: kubectl get svc (Should see nodeport-svc).

Step 6: Access Your App
Open your web browser on your computer and go to:
http://localhost:30001

You should see the "Welcome to nginx!" page.
